// U5 L4 Reproductive Health — 30-question bank

::U5L4 Q01::An STI is {=an infection that can spread through sexual contact ~a moral judgment ~always visible ~always cured with antibiotics}

::U5L4 Q02::Asymptomatic means {=present without noticeable symptoms ~guaranteed to be harmless ~visible by appearance ~not testable}

::U5L4 Q03::Maintained abstinence from sexual intercourse {=prevents pregnancy resulting from intercourse ~makes health information unnecessary ~proves consent in every relationship ~is determined by peer opinion}

::U5L4 Q04::Maintained abstinence from relevant sexual exposure {=avoids sexual transmission through that avoided exposure ~prevents every nonsexual transmission route ~requires personal disclosure ~eliminates the need for boundaries}

::U5L4 Q05::Risk reduction means {=lowering risk without necessarily eliminating it ~guaranteeing zero risk ~ignoring evidence ~self-diagnosing}

::U5L4 Q06::Which statement is accurate? {=Some pregnancy-prevention methods do not protect against STIs ~Every contraceptive method prevents STIs ~Appearance reveals STI status ~No symptoms prove no infection}

::U5L4 Q07::Condoms or barrier methods can {=reduce pregnancy risk and risk of many STIs when used correctly and consistently ~eliminate every possible risk ~replace professional testing ~make consent unnecessary}

::U5L4 Q08::Which source is most credible? {=A qualified public-health agency or healthcare professional ~An anonymous forum ~A product advertisement ~An unsourced viral post}

::U5L4 Q09::Consent must be {=voluntary and free from coercion ~assumed from silence ~obtained through repeated pressure ~permanent once given}

::U5L4 Q10::Coercion includes {=pressure, threats, or manipulation ~a respected boundary ~accurate health information ~professional testing}

::U5L4 Q11::A strong refusal sequence is {=state the boundary, repeat it, disengage, and seek support if needed ~debate until the other person agrees ~share private medical details ~ignore continued pressure}

::U5L4 Q12::A person without STI symptoms {=may still need testing after possible exposure ~can be assumed infection-free ~should rely on appearance ~cannot transmit infection}

::U5L4 Q13::The appropriate response to a possible STI exposure is {=seek qualified professional testing or care ~use an online symptom quiz as diagnosis ~judge the person involved ~wait for social-media advice}

::U5L4 Q14::Which statement about antibiotics is accurate? {=They do not treat viral infections ~They cure every STI ~They replace testing ~They prevent pregnancy}

::U5L4 Q15::HIV primarily affects the {=immune system ~skeletal system only ~hair and nails only ~digestive system only}

::U5L4 Q16::Which statement about HPV is accurate? {=It includes many types, some linked to health problems ~It is always visible ~It is a pregnancy-prevention method ~It proves irresponsible behavior}

::U5L4 Q17::Why is testing different from self-diagnosis? {=Testing uses appropriate professional procedures and interpretation ~Testing relies on appearance ~Self-diagnosis is always more accurate ~They are identical}

::U5L4 Q18::A student chooses abstinence despite peer pressure. The safest conclusion is {=the boundary should be respected and needs no lengthy justification ~peers should decide ~pressure creates consent ~the student lacks health knowledge}

::U5L4 Q19::A partner says, “If you cared, you would change your mind.” This is {=emotional pressure, not healthy consent ~proof of respect ~credible health information ~professional counseling}

::U5L4 Q20::Which claim confuses pregnancy and STI prevention? {=Any method that reduces pregnancy risk automatically prevents STIs ~Different methods address different risks ~Abstinence avoids relevant exposure when maintained ~Testing may be needed without symptoms}

::U5L4 Q21::Why is reproductive-health information useful to a student choosing abstinence? {=It supports informed boundaries, misinformation detection, and future healthcare decisions ~It requires personal disclosure ~It weakens abstinence ~It replaces consent}

::U5L4 Q22::Which response is non-stigmatizing? {=An STI is a health condition requiring factual support and care ~An STI proves bad character ~Pregnancy status determines worth ~People should be shamed into treatment}

::U5L4 Q23::Alcohol or drug impairment may affect {=judgment, safety, and the ability to consent ~the accuracy of all online posts ~whether coercion is acceptable ~whether boundaries matter}

::U5L4 Q24::The first step in the MLA decision process is to {=identify the decision or health issue ~accept peer pressure ~choose before checking facts ~post private information online}

::U5L4 Q25::Which evidence best challenges “everyone is doing it”? {=Perceived norms do not determine an individual's boundary or health choice ~Peers always know private behavior ~Popularity proves safety ~Pressure improves consent}

::U5L4 Q26::A method reduces pregnancy risk but offers no STI protection. It addresses {=pregnancy risk only ~STI risk only ~neither risk ~consent automatically}

::U5L4 Q27::A barrier method may reduce pregnancy and many STI risks. This addresses {=both risks, without guaranteeing zero risk ~only pregnancy under all conditions ~only STI risk under all conditions ~every possible health risk}

::U5L4 Q28::Which conclusion follows from an unreliable anonymous source promising instant diagnosis? {=Reject the claim and seek professional testing when appropriate ~Accept it because it is fast ~Share it as medical advice ~Avoid qualified care}

::U5L4 Q29::When pressure continues after a clear refusal, the next safe step is to {=leave or disengage and seek trusted support ~offer more private information ~treat silence as consent ~ignore safety}

::U5L4 Q30::Which analysis shows strongest critical thinking? {=It identifies facts and influence, distinguishes risks, evaluates sources, applies boundaries, and justifies professional support ~It repeats a slogan without evidence ~It diagnoses from symptoms ~It judges a person's worth}
